Phase D is "venue truth at $100, recorded drill" and THERE WAS NO DRILL.
There was a list of steps in prose, with nothing checking they happened or
happened in order. That is the distance between a plan and an asset: a drill
that lives in a markdown file gets half-run at 2am by somebody tired, and
its evidence is a scrollback nobody kept.

tools/drill.py is twelve stages. Each produces EVIDENCE — a number the venue
actually returned, never a checkmark — and it refuses to advance when one
fails:

  reachability  flat  venue_rules  fee_tier  inventory  warmup  margin  cap
  open  pair  unwind  final_reconcile  statement

`pair` and `final_reconcile` check against the VENUE, not against what we
just wrote down. `statement` comes out of the 0044 journal.

SAFE BY DEFAULT. Without --arm it reads and sends nothing. With it, it goes
through the same order gate as every other path, and it has NO ORDER PATH OF
ITS OWN: every order goes through CarryEngine and the broker it was handed,
which in production is build_bot's LedgerBroker around a CarryBroker. Tests
assert it constructs no client, never touches _request, and that the CLI
takes its broker from build_bot.

TESTED AGAINST A VENUE THAT MISBEHAVES ON COMMAND, because the sequence has
to be right BEFORE it meets a real one: unreachable venue, empty wallet,
inventory below one lot, thin margin, a position already there, a notional
above the cap, a perp leg that never lands, an unwind that will not fill.
The first run against Bybit will be the hundredth run of this sequence.

The worst outcome has its own test. When the unwind does not fill the
verdict is FAILED, still_open is true, and next_action reads "A HUMAN MUST
ACT NOW: the book is not flat... Nothing automated will retry."

WHAT THE DRILL FOUND BEFORE TOUCHING A VENUE (D28). A freshly started book
is blind for 24 HOURS. evaluate_entry refuses below EWMA_MIN_PRINTS settled
prints, prints come every eight hours, so a new deploy — new volume, first
run, a machine the host moved — stood aside with
INSUFFICIENT_FUNDING_HISTORY for a full day while /v5/market/funding/history
had been returning the last two hundred prints the whole time. The armed
drill hit it on its first run and could not open at all.

warm_funding_history() seeds the EWMA from SETTLED prints and
carry_cold_start reads them before the first tick. No look-ahead: every one
is already settled and already paid, exactly what on_candle would have
recorded had the process been running. It refuses to overwrite a restored
history — a restart that read its own ledger knows more than the venue's
last eight — and the newest stamp becomes the watermark so a settled print
is not booked again. A venue that will not hand them over is not a reason to
refuse to start; it logs and the book waits the day.

THE REPOSITORY. Deleted README.md — deliberately no front page; the repo
does not explain itself to whoever finds it, and an empty one had already
come back from a web edit unnoticed. Deleted bootstrap.sh and 24 MB of the
zip-blob repo this one replaced; that job is done, the source is in bot/
under one commit per fix, and git history holds the blobs.
tests/test_repo_shape.py asserts none of it returns, and that no live code
still expects it — a citation in a provenance docstring is a record, not a
dependency, and is allowed.

